  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1134 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge which has been our main weather influence \nin the past few days will continue to move slowly east into the \nLower Mississippi Valley on Sunday. To our west an upper low will \nmove from the Arizona/Utah border to northeastern Utah, and a \ntrough will extend south from this low along the NM/AZ border by \nSunday evening. Showers and a few thunderstorms associated with \nthis system will affect western and far northwestern parts of \nTexas on Sunday. A few showers may develop a little farther east \nand affect our western counties Sunday afternoon and evening, \nmainly west of a Sweetwater to Sterling City to Ozona line. For \nthe rest of our area, mostly cloudy skies in the morning will \nbecome partly cloudy in the afternoon. Highs will be mostly 76-81 \ndegrees with a south to southeast breeze. \n\nThe aforementioned upper low will open into a wave as the system \nlifts northeast into the central High Plains on Monday. A \nshortwave trough is forecast to move northeast into our area on \nMonday, possibly followed by another disturbance Tuesday. With \nfairly moist south-southeast low-level flow and precipitable water\nvalues at or above 1 inch, our area will have a possibility of \nisolated to scattered showers and a few thunderstorms. Expect a \nreduced diurnal temperature range with considerable cloud cover, \nand highs Monday and Tuesday are expected to be mostly in the mid \nto upper 70s. \n\nConsiderable cloud cover is expected again Wednesday with moist \nsouth-southeast low-level flow, but with a lack of upper support \nand surface focus, our area should be free of rain chances. The \ndeterministic medium range models differ on the details of how the\npattern will evolve late next week. But model ensembles support \nthe return of rain chances Thursday into Friday, when southwest \nflow aloft develops over our area ahead of a large scale trough \ndeveloping along the West Coast. Highs in the upper 70s to lower \n80s Wednesday will trend down to the lower and middle 70s by \nFriday. Nighttime lows will be mild and above normal, in the mid \n50s to around 60.",
